; The NAMED-PAYLOAD spelling of a nested `.cbor` — `inner = bytes .cbor uint` referenced as
; `bytes .cbor inner` — in top-level rule-BODY position. SUPPORTED: naming the inner payload is what
; gives it a boundary.
;
; A `.cbor` rule body mints a wrapper struct (the same one the `@newtype` spelling mints), so `Inner`
; is a real type with its own serialize fn and its own payload buffer, and `B` writes `43 41 05` for
; the value 5 — a byte string holding a byte string holding a uint. The reference crosses a TYPE
; rather than copying an encoding chain, which is why nothing here shares the `<var>_inner_se` buffer
; that makes the inline sibling (tests/robustness/cbor_payload_same_chain_inline.cddl) unbuildable.
;
; This fixture is the CONTROL for that sibling's refusal: the refusal keys on the same-chain
; composition, and a key that caught the named spelling too would refuse the documented remedy. It
; also pins the rule-body force-wrap from the outside — were `inner` to register as a transparent
; alias again, its own `.cbor` would ride on the `RustType` a reference copies whole, the chain would
; be shared, and this file would go back to being an error.
inner = bytes .cbor uint
b = bytes .cbor inner
